  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n| ${({J}_{2}(5))}^{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{\u2032}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\lambda )}{2!} & \u22ef & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{\u2032}(\\lambda ) & \u22ef & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ \u22ee & \u22f1 & \u22f1 & \u22f1 & \u22ee \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{\u2032}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & \u22ef & \u22ef & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)={x}^{3},$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =5.$\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivatives of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(5)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(5) & {f}^{\u2032}(5) \\\\ 0 & f(5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3} & 3\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}125 & 75 \\\\ 0 & 125\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
